From: mirx developers
Subject: [PATCH] gdi32/uniscribe: Do not share a script cache between threads.

init_script_cache hands out one ScriptCache per LOGFONT to every caller
in the process.  A ScriptCache is not just font metrics, though: it is
filled in lazily and without any locking as it is used (glyph and width
pages, the GSUB/GDEF/GPOS/CMAP tables, the OpenType script and feature
lists, which grow with usp10_array_reserve).  So two threads that shape
text with the same font, each with its own SCRIPT_CACHE variable, as
two EDIT controls on two threads do, race on the same ScriptCache and
corrupt the heap; for example both reallocate cache->scripts in
usp10_script_cache_add_script_list at once.

The code that guarded against two threads creating the same cache at
once was broken as well.  The thread that loses (whose cache is already
linked into script_cache_list, and which finds the other one) called
list_remove(&sc->entry) on the *other* cache, since the loop variable sc
pointed at it, and then freed its own cache while it was still linked.
The next lookup walked the freed node and typically faulted in
memcmp(&sc->lf, ...) at address 0x14, the offset of lf.

Only share a cache with callers on the thread that created it.  Then no
other thread can add a cache that matches between the lookup and the
insertion, so the broken race handling goes away rather than being
fixed.  A cache that an application itself passes to another thread is
still used from there; serialising such use is up to the application.

Seen as heap corruption and as crashes in ucrtbase!memcmp <-
init_script_cache <- ScriptStringAnalyse <- EDIT_UpdateUniscribeData
when several threads each put text into EDIT controls using the same
font.  N threads that each loop over ScriptStringAnalyse on a DC with
DEFAULT_GUI_FONT crash on every run without this patch.

---
--- a/dlls/gdi32/uniscribe/usp10.c
+++ b/dlls/gdi32/uniscribe/usp10.c
@@ -778,6 +778,7 @@
 
 static HRESULT init_script_cache(const HDC hdc, SCRIPT_CACHE *psc)
 {
+    DWORD thread_id = GetCurrentThreadId();
     ScriptCache *sc;
     unsigned size;
     LOGFONTW lf;
@@ -794,10 +795,12 @@
     size = lstrlenW(lf.lfFaceName);
     memset(lf.lfFaceName + size, 0, sizeof(lf.lfFaceName) - size * sizeof(WCHAR));
 
+    /* A cache fills itself in lazily and without locking, so it is only shared
+     * with callers on the thread that created it. */
     EnterCriticalSection(&cs_script_cache);
     LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(sc, &script_cache_list, ScriptCache, entry)
     {
-        if (!memcmp(&sc->lf, &lf, sizeof(lf)))
+        if (sc->thread_id == thread_id && !memcmp(&sc->lf, &lf, sizeof(lf)))
         {
             sc->refcount++;
             LeaveCriticalSection(&cs_script_cache);
@@ -826,25 +829,15 @@
         free(sc);
         return E_INVALIDARG;
     }
+    sc->thread_id = thread_id;
     sc->lf = lf;
     sc->refcount = 1;
     *psc = sc;
 
+    /* Only this thread adds caches with this thread_id, so none was added
+     * since the lookup above. */
     EnterCriticalSection(&cs_script_cache);
     list_add_head(&script_cache_list, &sc->entry);
-    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(sc, &script_cache_list, ScriptCache, entry)
-    {
-        if (sc != *psc && !memcmp(&sc->lf, &lf, sizeof(lf)))
-        {
-            /* Another thread won the race. Use their cache instead of ours */
-            list_remove(&sc->entry);
-            sc->refcount++;
-            LeaveCriticalSection(&cs_script_cache);
-            free(*psc);
-            *psc = sc;
-            return S_OK;
-        }
-    }
     LeaveCriticalSection(&cs_script_cache);
     TRACE("<- %p\n", sc);
     return S_OK;
--- a/dlls/gdi32/uniscribe/usp10_internal.h
+++ b/dlls/gdi32/uniscribe/usp10_internal.h
@@ -188,6 +188,7 @@
 typedef struct {
     struct list entry;
     DWORD refcount;
+    DWORD thread_id;
     LOGFONTW lf;
     TEXTMETRICW tm;
     OUTLINETEXTMETRICW *otm;
